  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Costco Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-costco-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Bobby Approved Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For shoppers who saw the Bobby Approved stamp, or heard Bobby Parrish talk protein in a Costco haul, and want to know what the powder is, what is in it, and where to buy it.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-is-it">What Bobby Approved Means</a>
      <a href="#costco">Is It at Costco?</a>
      <a href="#ingredients">What Is in It?</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs-costco">Vs Costco Powders</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Bobby Approved protein powder is FlavCity's All-in-One Protein Smoothie, the grass-fed whey and collagen mix that carries Bobby Parrish's Bobby Approved stamp on the back of the bag. It is sold on ShopFlavCity.com, Amazon, Target and TikTok Shop, not at Costco. Below: the label, the testing question, the Prop 65 warning, cost per 25 g, and the Costco powders to buy instead.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Bobby Approved Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bobby Approved protein powder is the FlavCity All-in-One Protein Smoothie, and it is not sold at Costco. The Bobby Approved stamp is printed on the back of the smoothie bag, and FlavCity's own list of authorized retailers names only its website, Amazon, Target and TikTok Shop, plus a store locator. Per the chocolate label at the time of writing, one scoop gives 25 g of protein and 200 calories, of which about 10 g of the protein appears to be collagen.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a stevia-sweetened, grass-fed breakfast smoothie you can shake in 30 seconds and you trust Bobby Parrish's ingredient rules. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want the cheapest complete protein for muscle gain, need a third-party certification, or were hoping to grab it on a Costco run. For that, our <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/">best Costco protein powder</a> guide ranks what the warehouse actually stocks.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers (Chocolate label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 49.5 g scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~10 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Of it from collagen</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.55</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Bobby Approved Powder and the Costco Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two cards cover the Bobby Approved smoothie itself, which ships from FlavCity's Amazon store. The other two are wheys Costco has carried that also skip artificial sweeteners, for readers who searched for a Bobby Approved powder at Costco and want something on that shelf. We have not confirmed how the Bobby Approved app scores the two Costco wheys, so scan them yourself before you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Bobby Approved Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            "Bobby Approved" is a label, not a separate brand of protein powder. Bobby Parrish, the FlavCity founder known for reading grocery labels aloud on YouTube, uses the phrase as his seal for products that pass his ingredient rules. It shows up in three places: as a round stamp on the back of FlavCity's own products, as the name of his free barcode-scanning app, and as the title of the shopping lists he shares from store hauls, Costco among them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The only protein powder that carries the Bobby Approved stamp on its own packaging is the FlavCity All-in-One Protein Smoothie. The back of the chocolate bag shows the round "Bobby Approved" badge beside the mixing instructions, and FlavCity's product photos show the same stamp next to the Vanilla Cream bag. The bag names FlavCity Health, LLC of Chicago as the trademark holder. If you have seen the phrase on a smoothie bag at Target or in an Amazon listing, this is the product. Our full <a href="/flavcity-protein-powder-review/">FlavCity protein powder review</a> covers the rest of the brand's line, including the plant-based smoothies and the single-serve packets.
          </p>

          <p>
            The app is the other half of the name. Per a review published by the rival Olive app, Bobby Approved is free on iOS and Android, scores products against ten principles that include no seed oils, no refined or artificial sugars, no GMOs, no harmful preservatives and no artificial colors, and highlights more than 100 flagged ingredients in red. The same review notes that the research behind the flagged list is not published. That matters: a Bobby Approved rating is one person's ingredient philosophy, not a lab result.
          </p>

          <p>
            So when someone searches "Bobby Approved protein powder," they usually mean one of two things. Either they want the FlavCity smoothie with the stamp on it, or they want to know which protein powders on a given store's shelf would pass Bobby's rules. This page answers both: the first through the FlavCity label, the second through the Costco wheys that skip sucralose and other artificial sweeteners.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE KEY POINT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">The stamp is an ingredient opinion, not a test</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Bobby Approved tells you a product avoids the ingredients Bobby Parrish dislikes. It does not tell you the powder was tested for heavy metals, banned substances or label accuracy. Only a certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice does that.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="costco" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Bobby Approved Protein Powder Sold at Costco?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. As of November 2026, FlavCity's authorized-retailer page lists ShopFlavCity.com, Amazon, Target and TikTok Shop, and Costco is not on it. Our searches turned up no FlavCity listing at Costco either. FlavCity warns buyers to stick to its approved retailers, so a FlavCity bag sold through some other channel is not one the brand stands behind.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Costco link in searches comes from Bobby Parrish himself. His videos have long included Costco grocery hauls in which he picks the items he would buy, and the Bobby Approved app lets users browse approved products by store, with Costco listed alongside Whole Foods, Aldi and Trader Joe's. That produces a steady stream of "Bobby Approved Costco" lists online, and some of them include protein powders. None of those powders is a Bobby Approved brand; they are other companies' products that he or his app rated well.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not see which Costco protein powders the app currently approves, and app ratings change as formulas change, so we will not guess. What we can do is apply the rules the app publishes to the powders Costco has carried. Kirkland Signature Whey is sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, per our <a href="/kirkland-protein-powder-review/">Kirkland protein powder review</a>, and Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard uses the same pair, so both would trip the artificial-sweetener rule. Ascent's whey uses stevia and Levels uses natural sweeteners, which puts them closer to Bobby's standard.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are standing in a Costco aisle, the practical move is to scan the tub with the app and read the ingredient list yourself. Stock varies by warehouse, Costco rotates protein brands every few months, and the same brand can sell a different flavor in-store and online. Our <a href="/aldi-vs-costco-protein-powder/">Aldi vs Costco protein powder</a> comparison shows how much warehouse selections differ, and the <a href="/sams-club-protein-powder/">Sam's Club protein powder</a> guide covers the other warehouse club.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sells FlavCity smoothies?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">ShopFlavCity.com</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-serving bags at $59.99, subscription discounts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Amazon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">FlavCity's store; $50.99 for 20 servings when we checked</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Target</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">In-store and online, 10-serving bags and a Target-only flavor, per FlavCity</td>
                  </tr>
