                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">TikTok Shop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">FlavCity's verified shop</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Costco</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not on FlavCity's authorized-retailer list</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in the Bobby Approved Protein Smoothie?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per the chocolate label at the time of writing, one 49.5 g scoop of the Bobby Approved smoothie gives 200 calories, 25 g of protein, 13 g of carbohydrate, 3 g of fiber, 3 g of total sugar with 0 g added, 4 g of fat, 1 g of saturated fat and 200 mg of sodium. There are 20 servings in a bag. That is a much bigger, more meal-like scoop than a plain whey, which usually runs 30 to 33 g for 120 calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            The printed ingredient list for chocolate reads: whey protein concentrate (grass-fed, undenatured), collagen (grass-fed), organic cocoa powder, coconut milk powder, milk protein concentrate (grass-fed), fermented nutritional yeast protein, banana powder, unrefined sea salt, organic stevia leaf extract (Reb A), stevia leaf extract (Reb M), organic cordyceps powder and organic reishi powder. It contains milk. The bag adds that it is made on shared equipment that may contain peanuts or tree nuts, which matters for anyone with a nut allergy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things on that list deserve attention. The first is collagen, the second ingredient. FlavCity says each scoop has 10 g of grass-fed bovine collagen, and the panel lists 25 g of total protein, so collagen appears to make up about 40% of the protein. Collagen lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, so it does far less for muscle repair than the same weight of whey. If you count only the whey, milk protein and yeast protein, the scoop gives roughly 15 g of complete protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is what is missing. There is no sucralose, acesulfame potassium, gum, carrageenan, seed oil or artificial color on the chocolate label, which is exactly what the Bobby Approved rules ask for. Sweetness comes from two stevia extracts and the banana and coconut powders. The reishi and cordyceps are there for FlavCity's focus and energy claims, which the brand makes without citing doses or studies, so treat them as flavor-neutral extras rather than a reason to buy.
          </p>

