        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Bobby Approved Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No third-party certification is named for the Bobby Approved smoothie. FlavCity's product pages, FAQ and packaging do not mention NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice, Informed Sport, the Clean Label Project or any other independent testing program, and our label-database check found no FlavCity listing in the NSF for Sport registry. The Bobby Approved stamp is Bobby Parrish's own seal, and it is applied to his own company's product, so it is not independent of the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bag also carries a California Proposition 65 warning: "Risk of reproductive harm from exposure to lead and cadmium." FlavCity prints the same warning on its product page. A Prop 65 notice means a product may expose the user to a listed chemical above California's very low safe-harbor level; it does not say how much lead or cadmium is in a scoop, and many cocoa and plant-heavy powders carry one. But it is worth knowing for a product sold on a clean-ingredient promise, and FlavCity does not publish heavy-metal results that would put a number on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cocoa is the usual source of lead and cadmium in chocolate protein powders, because the cacao plant absorbs both from soil. If heavy metals are your main concern, a vanilla or unflavored powder with published lab results is the safer bet. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide lists brands that publish their test results, and <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe?</a> explains what the warnings mean in practice.
          </p>

          <p>
            For athletes in tested sports, the answer is simple: without NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, this is not a powder to rely on. If you are pregnant, have kidney disease, take medication or are buying for an older adult with a medical condition, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before adding any protein supplement, and give them the full ingredient list, including the mushroom extracts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
