            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving to Costco to find it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                FlavCity's authorized-retailer list names its own site, Amazon, Target and TikTok Shop, not Costco. A Costco haul video or an app list can make it look like a warehouse item, but the Bobby Approved smoothie is not stocked there. Order it online or check Target, and buy a Costco whey if you are already in the aisle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the stamp as a safety test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bobby Approved means a product passes Bobby Parrish's ingredient rules. It is not a lab test for heavy metals, banned substances or label accuracy, and it sits on his own company's product. If testing matters to you, look for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice, which the FlavCity smoothie does not carry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting all 25 g as muscle protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                About 10 g of each scoop appears to be collagen, which lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine. For muscle repair, the useful figure is closer to 15 g of complete protein. If you are tracking protein for training, log the scoop as roughly 15 g or pair it with another protein source.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Prop 65 line on the back</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The bag warns of lead and cadmium exposure under California's Prop 65. That is not proof of a dangerous level, but FlavCity publishes no heavy-metal numbers to reassure you. Pregnant buyers and anyone drinking it daily should weigh that, and consider a vanilla or unflavored powder with published lab results.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying from an unlisted seller</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                FlavCity tells customers to buy only from its approved retailers. Third-party resellers on marketplaces can sell old stock, heat-damaged bags or fakes. On Amazon, buy from FlavCity's own store listing, and note that direct purchases are the only ones covered by FlavCity's one-time flavor swap.
              </p>
            </div>
